  nonce: 1d14  # 1d14
  message: "SCOPE CORRECTION BEFORE STARTING: the S5 sub-list in this item is now STALE.\nRe-checked every S5 row against the production loader on merged dev\n(e1d79d7315), not against the item's prose:\n\n  javascript WebSocket.onopen        ABSENT -- already removed (#606)\n  javascript WebSocket.onerror       ABSENT -- already removed (#606)\n  javascript EventSource.onerror     ABSENT -- already removed (#606)\n  haskell    Network.Socket.close    ABSENT -- already removed (INV-nular)\n  haskell    Network.Socket.gracefulClose  ABSENT -- already removed\n  javascript BroadcastChannel.postMessage  ALREADY CORRECTED -- it is ipc_send\n             today, not net_recv. The direction error this item records is\n             fixed; note the row is a SEND on an IPC channel, which is why the\n             WI-joruz direction sweep does not flag it.\n  javascript WebSocket.onclose       STILL LIVE (net_recv, method-kind)\n\nSo S5 reduces to ONE row, and that row is already inside WI-zumoz's population\n(WebSocket.onmessage, WebSocket.onclose, EventSource.onmessage). S5 should be\nFOLDED INTO WI-zumoz rather than worked here -- it is not a separate population\nany more.\n\nAND THE FOLD MAKES WI-zumoz's ANSWER EASIER, NOT HARDER. WebSocket.onclose is\nover-determined for deletion: it is a lifecycle callback carrying no\napplication data (this item's argument) AND structurally unmatchable, because\n`ws.onclose = handler` is a property ASSIGNMENT and a method-kind row can never\nmatch one (WI-zumoz's argument). Two independent arguments, same verdict.\n\nWHAT REMAINS IN THIS ITEM IS THE MAIN POPULATION, AND IT IS FULLY LIVE. All\neight builder rows still ship, all under net_send:\n  python     urllib.request.Request           net_send  function\n  javascript XMLHttpRequest.open              net_send  method\n  java       java.net.URL.openConnection      net_send  method\n  elixir     Req.new                          net_send  function\n  elixir     Finch.build                      net_send  function\n  go         crypto/tls.Client                net_send  function\n  haskell    Network.HTTP.Client.newManager   net_send  function\n  objc       NSMutableURLRequest.setHTTPBody: net_send  method\n\nProceeding with the item's own recommended step 1: adjudicate a sample of LIVE\nfindings at these rows, split by whether the tainted value is the resource\nSELECTOR or the PAYLOAD. Not deciding per row before that evidence exists --\nthis item's whole point is that the sink side is not F3's mirror, so a removal\nhere is the false-all-clear direction and needs more than a sweep."  # 1d14
- op: discuss  # 2c87
  at: '2026-08-28T22:37:09Z'  # 2c87
  by: agent  # 2c87
  actor: jgstern_agent  # 2c87
  clock: 4  # 2c87
  nonce: 2c87  # 2c87
  message: "STEP 1 DONE. Write-up: ~/hypergumbo_lab_notebook/lunav_newmanager_08282026/RESULT.md\nOutcome: ALL EIGHT ROWS STAY, and this item's own step 3 is mis-aimed.\n\n(A) THE LIVE FINDINGS CARRY NO ARGUMENT POSITION AT ALL. Every violated finding\nat a builder row in this corpus -- servant, 6 of 6 -- has analysis_method\nstructural, confidence approximate, source_taint None, and a path that is a\nSINGLE node, the enclosing function. The call sites are\n  mgr <- newManager defaultManagerSettings\n  defManager = unsafePerformIO $ C.newManager C.defaultManagerSettings\nwhere the argument is a LITERAL CONSTANT. No tainted value reaches it; the\nfinding fires because `main` also reads getArgs/lookupEnv and the sink sits in\nthe same function.\nCONSEQUENCE FOR THIS ITEM: step 3 proposes argument-position awareness in\ntaint.py. That would not change ONE of these findings, because none of them\nclaims an argument position. The mechanism here is function-granular\nattribution, which is a different defect from \"which parameter carried it\".\n\n(B) SELECTOR-VS-PAYLOAD IS MISSING THREE CLASSES, by API contract:\n  newManager        ManagerSettings only            -- transport CONFIG\n  URL.openConnection  ZERO arguments                -- selector via receiver only\n  XMLHttpRequest.open method,url,async,USER,PASSWORD -- selector + CREDENTIALS\n  crypto/tls.Client conn,*tls.Config -- SNI and client certs ARE transmitted\n  urllib.request.Request url, data=, headers=  (verified from installed stdlib)\n  Req.new / Finch.build  url, headers, body\n  setHTTPBody:      the body, definitionally       -- PAYLOAD only\nThe two omitted classes: CREDENTIALS/HEADERS (a token in headers= is\ntransmitted and is neither selector nor body -- the same asymmetry that put\nPlug's put_resp_header on the send side in #609), and TRANSPORT CONFIG, which\nitself splits, since a timeout is never transmitted but SNI and a client cert\nare. So tls.Client cannot be ruled by signature at all.\n\n(C) MEASURED, AND IT SETTLES THE STRONGEST REMOVAL CANDIDATE. newManager creates\na connection pool, transmits nothing at call time, and ALL its executors\n(httpLbs/httpBS/httpNoBody/responseOpen/withResponse) are separately rowed, so\nthe F3 licence LOOKS satisfied. It is not. servant, per-arm cache, canary on the\nloaded row:\n    KEEP  HOSTSECRET-NO-NETWORK violated(3)   UNTRUSTED-NO-NETWORK violated(3)\n    DROP  HOSTSECRET-NO-NETWORK inconclusive(0)  UNTRUSTED-NO-NETWORK inconclusive(0)\nRemoval COLLAPSES BOTH VERDICTS. Executors being rowed is not sufficient:\nservant's example clients reach the network through withClientM / the servant\n`client` combinator, which is NOT catalogued, so once newManager goes there is\nno rowed sink left in those functions and the crossing is represented NOWHERE.\nDirection note: it lands on inconclusive, not confirmed -- a withholding loss\nrather than a false all-clear -- but still the wrong direction, and a change\nthat reports less needs more evidence than one that reports more.\n\n(D) THIS ITEM IS BLOCKED ON WORK FILED ELSEWHERE. The unlock is either\n  1. catalogue the missing EXECUTORS (servant's client/withClientM and the\n     equivalents), after which removing the builders leaves the crossing\n     represented -- but that is recall-ADDING and recall is band-stopped under\n     WI-sivuz; or\n  2. value-tracked rather than co-located findings at these sites, which is\n     INV-fumod's cross-function / object-identity gap.\nNo catalogue change shipped. Staying todo_hard."  # 2c87
